  "sourceNotes": {
    "raw": "師傅 (si1 fu2) is a respectful way to address service workers like bus drivers, taxi drivers, and mechanics. It literally means 'master' or 'skilled worker'. Using this shows politeness and acknowledgment of their expertise, similar to calling a chef 'chef' in English.\n\n唔該 (m4 goi1) means 'thank you' for services or 'excuse me' to get attention. This differs from 多謝 (do1 ze6), which thanks someone for gifts or favors. When asking a bus driver something, always start with 唔該. The phrase 問吓嘢 (man6 haa5 je5) means 'ask something' - 吓 makes the action casual and brief, like 'just asking'.\n\n呢架車 (ni1 gaa3 ce1) uses the classifier 架 (gaa3) for vehicles. Classifiers are mandatory before 'this/that' - saying 呢車 is wrong. 係 (hai6) works as 'to be' and often appears in pattern 係...嘅 for emphasis: 係去旺角嘅 emphasizes the destination is Mongkok.\n\n嘅 (ge3) at sentence end marks possession or creates adjective phrases. In 係去旺角嘅, it emphasizes the direction or purpose. Common mistake: forgetting 嘅 makes sentences sound incomplete in Cantonese, even though Mandarin often omits it."
  }
